            <ArticleTitle>Evidence for the role of spinal cord systems in Parkinson's disease-associated pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>171-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A patient with Parkinson's disease had severe levodopa-associated leg pain in a beginning-of-dose and peak-dose pattern. Local anesthetic block of the lumbar sympathetic chain or differential epidural block did not alter the pain. Spinal anesthesia abolished the pain. We postulate that levodopa-associated pain in Parkinson's disease either originates in or is mediated by spinal cord dopaminergic systems.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
